<p>The United States was still waiting on May 10 for Iran’s response to a proposal to end more than two months of fighting and open peace talks, after President Trump and Secretary of State Marco Rubio said Washington expected an answer soon. The proposal would first formally end the war, then leave harder issues — including Iran’s nuclear program, reopening the Strait of Hormuz and lifting the U.S. blockade of Iranian ports — for follow-up talks. The wait followed recent clashes around the Strait of Hormuz since a ceasefire began a month ago, including U.S. strikes on two Iranian-flagged tankers that Washington said were trying to breach its blockade and Iranian accusations that the United States violated the ceasefire. Iran’s Revolutionary Guard navy warned that any attack on Iranian tankers or commercial vessels would bring a “heavy assault” on a U.S. base in the region and enemy ships, according to Iranian state media carried by several reports. Tehran had not publicly issued a reply, and Foreign Ministry spokesperson Esmail Baghaei told Tasnim that the proposal was “under review,” while Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said U.S. actions in the Gulf had deepened doubts about Washington’s motives and seriousness in diplomacy. Rubio met Qatari Prime Minister Mohammed bin Abdulrahman al-Thani in Miami on May 9 to discuss regional security, and reports said a Qatari tanker was sailing toward the strait.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/b85G1saEpDs-MtIIr1zRolR72gWYR8pQYrWimBQkhZSNVdGMZgHMX_wegPk19wWp8f5yp3LHhn5Af_IKZZSAsrltls9tM2dqJABFK3p5DRD8swPUYT0Ciw3PK7Nho1b6-UwKFSKIdJUjOrsr42baqZU5HzU1o6l_VgIsbkkwEjGFRzSvOPWRcbXie_f5-MT4E9bFmVLiIJ3oZzH1Uou5RCqEl8dc5G45kaJo8Ly4rcmXrwyBehuYF25KljpuBzHqSbgTzpQ0sA' alt='US guided-missile destroyer USS Rafael Peralta shadows the Iran-flagged crude oil tanker vessel Stream as part of the US blockade of Iranian ports.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Shipping route: Before the war, about one-fifth of the world’s oil supply moved through the Strait of Hormuz, and Tehran has largely blocked non-Iranian shipping there since the conflict began.</li><li>Blockade pressure: A CIA assessment cited in reports said Iran could withstand the U.S. naval blockade for months.</li><li>Military option: President Trump said the United States could return to an expanded version of the paused “Project Freedom” naval operation if the Iran deal is not completed.</li></ul><blockquote>Any attack on Iranian tankers and commercial vessels will result in a heavy attack on one of the American centres in the region and enemy ships - Iran’s Revolutionary Guards</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: Trump told reporters at the White House that he still expected Iran’s response “tonight” and, when asked whether Tehran was slow-walking the process, said, “we’ll find out soon enough.” (<a href='https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2026-05-09/us-awaits-response-after-hormuz-clashes-strain-ceasefire-video'>Bloomberg</a>)</li><li>U.S. State Department: The State Department said Rubio and Qatar’s prime minister discussed continuing cooperation “to deter threats and promote stability and security across the Middle East,” without mentioning Iran in its statement. (<a href='https://www.rappler.com/world/middle-east/usa-iran-war-updates-may-10-2026/'>Rappler</a>)</li><li>Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi: Araghchi told his Turkish counterpart that recent U.S. actions in the Persian Gulf and alleged ceasefire violations had increased Iranian suspicions about Washington’s diplomatic intent. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1999055/iran-keeps-us-waiting-for-a-response'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>U.S. Central Command: CENTCOM commander Brad Cooper said U.S. forces in the Middle East remained committed to enforcing the blockade of vessels entering or leaving Iran. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/us-forces-strike-iran-oil-tankers-while-waiting-tehran-peace-proposal/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li